  "narrative": {
    "intro": "DCS has $217M in contract obligations in FY2026 to date. DCS secured 73 awards so far, with an average award size of $3M. Obligations are down 43.2% year over year. Yearly obligations show a peak of $382M in FY25 and a low of $217M in FY26 to date. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $216M. The General Services Administration obligated $891K. Engineering Services is the largest market for DCS at $128M, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $53M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) at $26.4M. Other FY2026 markets include Custom Computer Programming Services at $9.2M, Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $264K, and Aircraft Manufacturing at $0. These figures are fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations unless otherwise noted.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did DCS perform in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Total obligations are $217M across 73 awards, with an average award size of $3M. Obligations fell 43.2% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows obligations ranged from $326M in FY21 to $382M in FY25, with $217M in FY26 to date. The Department of Defense is the primary source of obligations at $216M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who buys from DCS and what does DCS sell?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense and the General Services Administration are the top buying agencies, with $216M and $891K in obligations respectively. Engineering Services is the largest market at $128M. Research and development markets account for $53M and $26.4M respectively, and Custom Computer Programming Services accounts for $9.2M. Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services account for $264K, and Aircraft Manufacturing accounts for $0."
      }
    ]
  },
